<com:TContent ID="body" >
<h1>Extensions (Composer Plugins)</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.2.0" />

<p class="block-content">
A PRADO extension adds functionality to an application through a Composer package. An
extension provides new controls, services, behaviors, command-line actions, or pages.
Extensions are Composer packages of the type <tt>prado4-extension</tt>, and the
community publishes them on Packagist.
</p>

<h2>What an extension is</h2>
<p class="block-content">
An extension is a Composer package that names a bootstrap module in the
<tt>extra.bootstrap</tt> field of its <tt>composer.json</tt>. The application loads the
package as a module whose id is the package name. The bootstrap module is the entry
point of the extension, and it registers whatever the extension provides.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="json" CssClass="source block-content">
{
  "name": "myvendor/myplugin",
  "type": "prado4-extension",
  "keywords": ["prado", "framework", "extension"],
  "require": {
    "php": ">=8.1.0",
    "pradosoft/prado": "^4.3"
  },
  "repositories": [
    { "type": "composer", "url": "https://asset-packagist.org" }
  ],
  "autoload": {
    "psr-4": { "MyVendor\\MyPlugin\\": "src/" }
  },
  "extra": {
    "bootstrap": "MyVendor\\MyPlugin\\TMyPluginModule"
  }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The extension's classes load through Composer's PSR-4 autoloading. The
<tt>autoload.psr-4</tt> field maps a namespace prefix to a directory, so a class such as
<tt>MyVendor\MyPlugin\TMyPluginModule</tt> loads from <tt>src/TMyPluginModule.php</tt>.
Give the extension its own vendor and package namespace, so its fully qualified class
names do not collide with the application or with another extension.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>require</tt> field sets the PHP version and the <tt>pradosoft/prado</tt> version
the extension needs, along with any other packages it uses. The <tt>type</tt> is
<tt>prado4-extension</tt>, and the <tt>keywords</tt> help others find the extension on
Packagist, where PRADO extensions are listed by that type.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
PRADO depends on front-end asset packages, such as jQuery, that are distributed through
the asset-packagist repository. A project that installs PRADO adds this repository so
Composer resolves the assets, and an extension adds it as well to install on its own.
</p>

<h2>Installing and enabling an extension</h2>
<p class="block-content">
Install an extension with Composer. The application reads the installed packages and
finds the extensions among them.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
composer require myvendor/myplugin
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Enable the extension by declaring a module whose id is the package name. The module has
no <tt>class</tt> attribute, because the class comes from the package's
<tt>extra.bootstrap</tt> field.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;modules&gt;
  &lt;module id="myvendor/myplugin" /&gt;
&lt;/modules&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>The plugin module</h2>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TPluginModule</tt> is the base class for an extension's bootstrap module. It serves
the pages found in a <tt>Pages</tt> directory inside the package, and it registers the
package's <tt>errorMessages.txt</tt> file with the exception system. A subclass gains
these capabilities by extending <tt>TPluginModule</tt>.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
namespace MyVendor\MyPlugin;

class TMyPluginModule extends \Prado\Util\TPluginModule
{
    // pages under the package's Pages/ directory are served automatically
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<h3>Where the pages live</h3>

<p class="block-content">
The module finds its own directory by reflecting on its class file, and looks for a
<tt>Pages</tt> directory beside it. A lowercase <tt>pages</tt> is accepted as well. Three
properties control this.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>PluginPath</tt></td>
	<td>The package's directory. It defaults to the directory holding the bootstrap class, which is right for a package laid out in the usual way.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>RelativePagesPath</tt></td>
	<td>Where the pages sit relative to that, defaulting to <tt>Pages</tt>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>PluginPagesPath</tt></td>
	<td>The resolved absolute path. Setting it directly overrides the other two.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>How a plugin page becomes reachable</h3>

<p class="block-content">
Plugin pages are not copied into the application's page tree. On each request the module
attaches a handler to the page service's <tt>onAdditionalPagePaths</tt> event, and that
handler offers the package's own pages directory as another place to look. A page the
application does not have is then found in the package.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Because the page renders inside the application's own layout, it cannot name a content
placeholder of its own. It reads the id from an application parameter instead, so one
setting adapts every plugin page to whatever the application's master template calls its
main region.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TContent ID=&lt;%$ PluginContentId %&gt;&gt;
  ...
&lt;/com:TContent&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
The application declares the parameter once, naming the placeholder its layout uses.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;parameters&gt;
  &lt;parameter id="PluginContentId" value="Main" /&gt;
&lt;/parameters&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h3>Error messages</h3>

<p class="block-content">
An <tt>errorMessages.txt</tt> file at the root of the package is registered with the
exception system when the module initializes, so the extension raises exceptions carrying
its own messages. The file is optional and is ignored when absent.
</p>

<h2>Command line actions</h2>

<p class="block-content">
An extension adds commands to the <a href="?page=GettingStarted.CommandLine">command line
tool</a> by registering an action class on the shell application. The registration happens
only when the application is a shell application, so the same module works unchanged under
a web request.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function registerShellAction($sender, $param)
{
    $app = $this-&gt;getApplication();
    if ($app instanceof \Prado\Shell\TShellApplication) {
        $app-&gt;addShellActionClass([
            'class' =&gt; MyPluginAction::class,
            'PluginModule' =&gt; $this,
        ]);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
The array form names the action class and sets properties on it, which is how the action
reaches back to the module that registered it. The action class extends
<tt>TShellAction</tt> and declares the commands it answers to. Several shipped modules use
this route, including the ones behind the cron, permissions and database parameter
commands.
</p>

<h2>Database extensions</h2>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TDbPluginModule</tt> extends <tt>TPluginModule</tt> for an extension that needs a
database connection. The <tt>ConnectionID</tt> property names a <tt>TDataSourceConfig</tt>
module for the connection. The connection cannot change after the module initializes, so
a single application parameter configures every database extension the same way.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;modules&gt;
  &lt;module id="db" class="Prado\Data\TDataSourceConfig"&gt;
    &lt;database ConnectionString="mysql:host=localhost;dbname=mydb"
              Username="dbuser" Password="dbpass" /&gt;
  &lt;/module&gt;
  &lt;module id="myvendor/mydbplugin" ConnectionID="db" /&gt;
&lt;/modules&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

</com:TContent>
